1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,800
I want you to imagine, just for a moment, learning quantum mechanics.

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:09,480
And I don't mean the way it is usually taught to, like, a terrified college sophomore.

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:14,760
Right. Yeah, the traditional pedagogical approach is very much an exercise in blunt force.

4
00:00:15,020 --> 00:00:19,100
It's about handing you the strange counterintuitive rules of the quantum world.

5
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:19,380
Yeah.

6
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:22,100
And just saying, you know, here is how reality operates.

7
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:22,600
Exactly.

8
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:29,600
You walk into a physics classroom and the professor immediately starts throwing these bizarre magical concepts at the whiteboard.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,620
Like, you get cats in boxes that are somehow miraculously alive and dead at the exact same time.

10
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:37,980
Which is always a fun one for the students.

11
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:38,500
Oh, for sure.

12
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:46,680
And you get particles communicating instantly across the vastness of the universe in what Einstein famously dismissed as spooky action at a distance.

13
00:00:46,940 --> 00:00:48,320
Yeah, he really hated that.

14
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:54,420
But you get these massive, heavy, mathematically dense postulates just dropping from the sky, right?

15
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:57,460
Telling you that the universe is just fundamentally weird at the bottom.

16
00:00:57,520 --> 00:00:58,960
And you just have to accept it and calculate.

17
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:00,780
It absolutely feels like magic.

18
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:03,540
But what if we completely flip the script on that?

19
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:06,880
I mean, that is a vastly more demanding way to look at the universe.

20
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:08,480
You aren't just asking what happens.

21
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:09,380
Well, you're asking why.

22
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:09,720
Exactly.

23
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:12,960
You're asking why must it happen this way and no other way.

24
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:17,660
But ultimately, it is a much more satisfying lens through which to view reality.

25
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:21,420
And that is the entire mission for today's Deep Dive.

26
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:27,760
You, the listener, are going to explore a truly fascinating text published in 2026.

27
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:35,600
It's titled, Quantum Mechanics from Admissible Probabilistic Composition by an Independent Researcher Named Flyxion.

28
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,060
It's quite a title.

29
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:37,680
It is.

30
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:43,260
But the guiding light we are going to follow today is a brilliantly simple phrase coined by the author.

31
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,860
Constraint before content.

32
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:50,460
Constraint before content is a beautiful philosophy for unpacking physics.

33
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:54,640
It means we are deliberately not going to talk about physical objects right away.

34
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,280
So no electrons or photons.

35
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:56,720
That's right.

36
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:01,380
We are banning the words electrons, photons, and energy levels from our vocabulary for the time being.

37
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:01,700
Yeah.

38
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,760
We are going to strip everything back to the absolute bare minimum.

39
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:08,540
Looking purely at the logical rules of moving information around, right?

40
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:09,540
Precisely.

41
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:15,020
Just the foundational constraints on how probabilities have to work when you compose systems.

42
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:23,900
And from those seemingly innocent constraints, we are going to watch the entirety of textbook quantum mechanics magically reconstruct itself.

43
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:25,840
Here's where it gets really interesting.

44
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:30,940
This text challenges the very foundation of how we think about the fabric of reality.

45
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:31,820
It really does.

46
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:40,140
Because it treats quantum math not as some weird curiosity that we stumbled upon in a laboratory experiment, but as the thing to be explained.

47
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:41,840
It adds a daring question.

48
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:47,480
Under what logical structural constraints does the universe have to use quantum mechanics to function?

49
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:52,860
And to answer a question that profound the author, takes us on a journey starting from absolute zero.

50
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:56,380
We have to begin with the most basic concept imaginable.

51
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:59,800
Let's look at a finite set of distinguishable alternatives.

52
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:01,900
Okay, so no quantum weirdness yet.

53
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:04,240
Just regular, everyday states of knowledge.

54
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:04,540
Right.

55
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,480
Think of flipping a standard coin.

56
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:08,720
It lands on heads or it lands on tails.

57
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:14,780
Or imagine a basic laboratory detector that either clicks when something hits it or just remains silent.

58
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:18,440
So we have a set of distinct mutually exclusive outcomes.

59
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:19,300
Exactly.

60
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:23,520
And we have a state of knowledge about which outcome is going to happen.

61
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:26,080
This is just a standard probability distribution.

62
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:30,180
A list of positive numbers that sum to exactly 100%.

63
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:38,880
And in mathematics, this space of all possible probability distributions for a given set of outcomes is called the probability simplex.

64
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:39,200
Right.

65
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:43,760
So if we visualize that for the coin flip, your simplex is just a simple straight line segment.

66
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:48,080
One end of the line is 100% heads, 0% tails.

67
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:51,120
And the other end is 0% heads, 100% tails.

68
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:51,800
You got it.

69
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:54,840
Any point on that line is a valid state of your knowledge.

70
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:59,080
You could be sitting right in the middle at a 50-50 state of maximum ignorance.

71
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:03,160
Or sitting closer to one end at like 70-30 if you think the coin is weighted.

72
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:03,740
Yeah.

73
00:04:03,820 --> 00:04:10,140
And those points at the very ends, the 100% certainties where you know exactly what will happen, those are called the extreme points of the simplex.

74
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,500
So far, we are dealing with pure classical logic.

75
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:13,940
Okay.

76
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:14,920
So what's the next step?

77
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:20,820
The next evolutionary step is to ask how do we mathematically transform one state of knowledge into another?

78
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:23,000
Like how does the system evolve?

79
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,200
Because things change over time.

80
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:26,900
Information updates.

81
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:27,600
Exactly.

82
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:33,440
In this bare bones framework, a transformation is represented by something called a stochastic map.

83
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:34,580
A stochastic map.

84
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:35,080
Okay.

85
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:42,380
If you imagine your probabilities as a vertical column of numbers, a stochastic map is a grid of numbers.

86
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:48,140
A matrix that multiplies your current probability distribution to update it into a new one.

87
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:54,080
The fundamental constraint here is that the columns of this matrix must sum to one.

88
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:58,080
That makes intuitive sense because probability has to be conserved right.

89
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:05,440
You can't put a 100% chance of something happening into a machine and have the machine spit out 120% total chance across all outcomes.

90
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:06,800
No, you definitely can't.

91
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:11,200
The universe ultimately has to land on one of the alternatives when all is said and done.

92
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:14,020
So the total probability must always anchor at one.

93
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,900
So the column normalization ensures exactly that.

94
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:23,620
If you feed the matrix a valid probability distribution, it guarantees you will get a valid probability distribution out the other side.

95
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:24,020
Right.

96
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:25,860
And the text highlights a key concept here.

97
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:33,920
If a transformation perfectly maps distinct inputs to distinct outputs without blurring them together, it's a deterministic map.

98
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,220
Like a clean transition.

99
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:35,920
Exactly.

100
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:44,060
These deterministic maps, these clean transitions, form the extreme corners of the set of all possible stochastic maps.

101
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:45,500
That feels very foundational.

102
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:50,960
We have probabilities and we have matrices that shuffle those probabilities from one state to another.

103
00:05:50,960 --> 00:06:00,260
But the author introduces a critical structural constraint, next right, moving from a standard stochastic map to something called a bistochastic map.

104
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:01,920
This is a major pivot in the logic.

105
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,020
A standard stochastic map only demands that the columns sum to one to conserve probability.

106
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:14,320
A bistochastic map, which is sometimes called a doubly stochastic map, demands that the columns in the rows sum to one.

107
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,040
Wait, why do we care about the rows?

108
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:18,740
Conserving probability down the columns makes physical sense.

109
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:22,860
But what physical reality does making the rows sum to one actually represent?

110
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:27,900
Requiring the rows to sum to one imposes a profound physical constraint on the system.

111
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:31,440
Reversibility and the preservation of maximum uncertainty.

112
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:32,760
Preservation of uncertainty.

113
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:33,360
Yeah.

114
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:43,760
If you take the uniform distribution, the state where everything is equally likely, like our perfectly fair 50-50 coin, and you apply a bistochastic map to it, you get the uniform distribution right back.

115
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:44,280
Oh, I see.

116
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:46,560
So the map preserves your complete ignorance.

117
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:47,120
Exactly.

118
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:54,960
If you start out knowing absolutely nothing about the outcome a bistochastic process guarantees, you will still know absolutely nothing after it happens.

119
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:56,400
Okay, let's unpack this.

120
00:06:56,920 --> 00:07:03,340
The author brings in a heavy-hitter mathematical theorem to explain what these bistochastic maps actually look like under the hood.

121
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:05,500
The Berkhoff-Von Neumann theorem.

122
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:07,980
It is a stunning piece of geometry.

123
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:22,320
The Berkhoff-Von Neumann theorem states that if you take every single possible bistochastic matrix and group them together, they form a highly specific geometric shape in multidimensional space called the Berkhoff polytope.

124
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:29,920
Just to make sure we are visualizing this correctly, a polytope is essentially a geometric shape with flat sides and sharp corners, right?

125
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:36,180
Like a 2D polygon or a 3D polyhedron, like a cube, but generalized into any number of higher dimensions.

126
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:38,080
That is a perfect visualization.

127
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:41,720
It's a closed shape defined by flat boundaries.

128
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:45,960
The theorem proves something extraordinary about this shape, though.

129
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:53,520
The extreme corners of the Berkhoff polytope, the sharpest points of the geometry, are exactly the classical permutation matrices.

130
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:59,620
Permutation matrices, by that we mean maps that do nothing but cleanly swap labels around, right?

131
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:00,320
Exactly.

132
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,200
Taking outcome A and calling it outcome B.

133
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,820
Taking outcome B and calling it outcome C.

134
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:08,360
Pure reversible label swapping.

135
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:08,740
Right.

136
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:20,120
The theorem proves that any bistochastic matrix, no matter how complicated or fuzzy it looks, can be written mathematically as a convex combination, a weighted average of these pure classical label swaps.

137
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:24,120
The text actually walks through the proof of this, and the mechanism is fascinating.

138
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:26,880
They use something called a bipartite support graph.

139
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:30,340
Yeah, they imagine drawing a set of dots for the rows and a set of dots for the columns.

140
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:36,220
And they draw lines connecting a row to a column if there's a non-zero probability sitting in that spot in matrix.

141
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:48,360
And the brilliant part is they prove that if the matrix isn't just a simple, clean label swap, this web of connections must contain a cycle, a closed loop.

142
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:51,780
That loop is the vulnerability that proof exploits.

143
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:58,760
Because there's a closed cycle of probabilities, you can slightly tweak the numbers running around that loop.

144
00:08:58,860 --> 00:09:00,100
Like shifting the values.

145
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:00,860
Right.

146
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:09,160
You can add a microscopic amount of probability to one connection and subtract that exact same amount from the next connection in the circle, rippling this change all the way around.

147
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:13,840
Oh, wow. So you just ripple a little plus epsilon, minus epsilon around the circle.

148
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:18,260
And because it's a closed loop, the row totals and the column totals never actually change.

149
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,040
They remain perfectly balanced at one.

150
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:21,400
You're hitting on the core mechanism.

151
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:30,000
If you can slightly tweak a matrix in two different opposing directions around this loop and still maintain a perfectly valid bistochastic matrix,

152
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,800
then geometrically that matrix cannot be an extreme corner of the shape.

153
00:09:34,860 --> 00:09:35,820
Because it has wiggle room.

154
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:43,260
Exactly. It has wiggle room, meaning it must be somewhere flat on a face or somewhere deep inside the volume of the polycope.

155
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:50,280
Therefore, the only matrices that are the extreme immovable corners are the ones with no loops at all.

156
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,240
The pure permutations.

157
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:59,500
So any process that preserves the uniform distribution is ultimately just a fuzzy weighted average of perfectly clean label swaps.

158
00:09:59,700 --> 00:09:59,820
Ah.

159
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:00,880
That's wild.

160
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:09,080
But then the text introduces another layer to this called majorization, specifically the Hardy Littlewood-Polya majorization concept.

161
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:14,080
This theorem connects our geometric bistochastic maps to the physical concept of mixing.

162
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:22,260
It states that if you apply a bistochastic map to any probability distribution, the resulting distribution is always majorized by the original one.

163
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:23,200
Majorized?

164
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:23,440
Right.

165
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:24,340
Meaning what, exactly?

166
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:28,460
In plain terms, the distribution is forced to become more uniform, more smeared out.

167
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:30,980
It can never become more concentrated or more certain.

168
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,880
Wait, making things more uniform, forcing a system to become more smeared out?

169
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:36,520
Isn't that just entropy increasing?

170
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:38,580
Are we secretly doing thermodynamics here?

171
00:10:38,620 --> 00:10:39,900
It sounds a lot like it, doesn't it?

172
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:49,760
Because if I take a perfectly ordered brand new deck of plain cards, Ace of Spades, on top two of spades, next, all the way down, that represents highly concentrated certain probability.

173
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:50,240
Right.

174
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:51,620
You know exactly where everything is.

175
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:57,000
But if I shuffle it, which is physically applying random permutations, it gets more mixed up.

176
00:10:57,080 --> 00:10:59,480
I am literally applying bistochastic maps.

177
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:05,580
And the deck gets closer and closer to a totally uniform, random state where any card could be anywhere.

178
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:07,960
That is the definition of entropy increasing.

179
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:11,140
The card shuffling analogy captures the mathematics perfectly.

180
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:15,840
But what's fascinating here is the author's vital clarification on why this is happening.

181
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:19,820
Yes, entropy cannot decrease under bistochastic evolution.

182
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:27,860
But in this framework, this is not a physical thermodynamic postulate that we arbitrarily stapled onto our math.

183
00:11:27,980 --> 00:11:28,120
Right.

184
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:33,980
We didn't just walk into the room, write the second law of thermodynamics on the chalkboard, and demand that the universe obey it.

185
00:11:34,340 --> 00:11:35,260
We didn't have to.

186
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:43,100
The non-decrease of entropy is a structural, inescapable consequence of the geometry of the Berkhoff polytope itself.

187
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:49,940
Because bistochastic maps are, by geometric definition, just weighted averages of permutations.

188
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:53,880
Applying them inherently mixes and dilutes the probability.

189
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:57,060
And mathematical structure forces the entropy to increase.

190
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:00,540
The thermodynamics falls out of the geometry completely for free.

191
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:00,980
Exactly.

192
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:04,500
That is deeply satisfying. It removes the magic from thermodynamics.

193
00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:09,200
But looking at the assumption ledger, the author keeps track of everything we've explored so far.

194
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:17,960
Probability simplices, bistochastic matrices, entropy increasing through permutations is perfectly 100% classical.

195
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:20,540
Yes, we are still just flipping coins and shuffling cards.

196
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:22,420
There is zero quantum mechanics happening.

197
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:23,240
None whatsoever.

198
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:27,260
A classical computer can simulate everything we have discussed with absolute perfection.

199
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:27,960
Entirely.

200
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:31,060
So what breaks this beautiful, intuitive classical picture?

201
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:39,060
What geometric crisis forces us to invent quantum amplitudes and leave the comfortable everyday world of classical probabilities behind?

202
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:43,240
That brings us to the first major fracture in the classical foundation.

203
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:46,000
This is where real numbers begin to fail us.

204
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Let's dive into chapter 3.

205
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:54,940
The author introduces two new terms to describe specific flavors of these bistochastic matrices we've been talking about.

206
00:12:56,060 --> 00:12:58,580
Orthostochastic and unistochastic.

207
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,380
Let's get these defined clearly for the listener.

208
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:04,540
We established what a bistochastic matrix is.

209
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:08,680
A grid of probabilities where both rows and columns sum to one.

210
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:11,000
But we have to ask a deeper question.

211
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:13,560
Where do those probabilities actually come from?

212
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:13,980
Right.

213
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:18,340
In textbook quantum mechanics, we know that probabilities are not the foundational layer.

214
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:22,680
Probabilities are generated by squaring underlying amplitudes.

215
00:13:22,880 --> 00:13:23,700
The famous Born rule.

216
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:28,780
You take the wave function, you square the absolute value, and boom, you have your probability of finding the particle.

217
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:29,960
That's the mechanism.

218
00:13:30,320 --> 00:13:36,640
So an orthostochastic matrix is one where the probabilities in the grid come from squaring the entries of a real orthogonal matrix.

219
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:42,700
Which means we are building our underlying amplitude layer using only real numbers, standard positive and negative numbers.

220
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:50,480
Exactly. And a unistochastic matrix is one where the probabilities come from the squared magnitudes of a unitary matrix.

221
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:54,860
This means we are building our amplitude layer using complex numbers.

222
00:13:54,980 --> 00:14:00,020
Numbers that contain imaginary parts which physically correspond to rotating phase angles.

223
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:05,120
Right. So to simplify orthostochastic means a process that can be built using only real numbers.

224
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:08,720
Unistochastic means a process that requires complex numbers.

225
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:14,340
And the million dollar question the text poses is, are these two sets of matrices actually different?

226
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:19,500
Like, does the universe care if we use real or complex numbers to build its probability transitions?

227
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:24,080
The author begins testing this by looking at the simplest possible system you could construct.

228
00:14:24,660 --> 00:14:27,280
A system with only two mutually exclusive outcomes.

229
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:29,040
An N equals 2 system.

230
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:32,060
Think of a single isolated quantum bit, a quibit.

231
00:14:32,060 --> 00:14:34,600
And what happens when we test the math at N equals 2?

232
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:38,520
At N equals 2, the mathematics simply cannot tell the difference between the two fields.

233
00:14:38,900 --> 00:14:41,300
The author proves the N equals 2 degeneracy theorem.

234
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:48,520
It demonstrates that every single 2 by 2 bistochastic matrix is simultaneously orthostochastic and unistochastic.

235
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:56,980
Yeah, any valid two-outcome probability transition you can dream up can be perfectly flawlessly explained using only real numbers.

236
00:14:56,980 --> 00:15:02,460
You absolutely do not need complex numbers to describe a single isolated two-level system.

237
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:09,180
So if the entire universe consisted of nothing but isolated coin flips, scientists would never have invented complex quantum mechanics.

238
00:15:09,580 --> 00:15:11,800
A purely real number theory works flawlessly.

239
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,100
This is entirely sufficient.

240
00:15:13,180 --> 00:15:16,600
But the universe is obviously vastly larger than a two-sided coin.

241
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,140
What happens when we add just one single additional outcome?

242
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:22,240
What happens when we move to N equals 3?

243
00:15:22,500 --> 00:15:26,320
At N equals 3, the classical real number picture violently shatters.

244
00:15:26,320 --> 00:15:31,160
The text presents the N greater than or equal to 3 incompleteness theorem.

245
00:15:31,580 --> 00:15:32,220
Sounds ominous.

246
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:33,780
It kind of is for classical math.

247
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:40,540
It proves mathematically that there are valid probability transitions that exist perfectly in the complex unistochastic world,

248
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:44,300
but are physically impossible to construct in the real orthostochastic world.

249
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:46,680
And the author doesn't just state this abstractly.

250
00:15:46,740 --> 00:15:50,140
They bring out a devastatingly concrete counterexample.

251
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:54,040
The 3 by 3 discrete Fourier transform matrix.

252
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:54,940
Yes.

253
00:15:55,560 --> 00:15:59,800
The DFT matrix is a cornerstone of complex mathematics and signal processing.

254
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:06,820
It's a 3 by 3 unitary matrix filled with complex phases, imaginary numbers representing different rotations.

255
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:08,960
Okay, so it's full of complex numbers.

256
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:09,600
Right.

257
00:16:09,700 --> 00:16:15,560
But if you take the absolute value squared of every single entry in this complex matrix, the phases disappear.

258
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:21,300
And you get a very simple 3 by 3 grid where every single entry is exactly one third.

259
00:16:21,660 --> 00:16:24,980
A perfectly valid, incredibly boring by stochastic matrix.

260
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:28,820
Just one third, one third, one third across every row and column.

261
00:16:29,080 --> 00:16:35,260
It represents a process that takes any state and completely randomizes it equally across the three outcomes.

262
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:35,780
Exactly.

263
00:16:35,940 --> 00:16:40,000
Because we derive this one third grid by squaring a complex unitary matrix.

264
00:16:40,120 --> 00:16:40,320
Right.

265
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:43,200
We know for a fact that it is unistochastic.

266
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:45,680
It happily exists in the complex quantum framework.

267
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:45,940
Right.

268
00:16:45,940 --> 00:16:52,880
The trap the author sets is asking, can we build this exact same one third grid from the bottom up using only real numbers?

269
00:16:53,040 --> 00:17:03,160
Meaning, can we find a 3 by 3 matrix of purely real numbers, positive and negative values, that are all orthogonal to each other, and when you square every number, they all equal one third?

270
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:04,440
Let's follow the logic.

271
00:17:05,340 --> 00:17:16,640
If you square a real number, and the answer is one third, the original number must have been either positive 1 over the square root of 3 or negative 1 over the square root of 3.

272
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:19,320
Because there are no other options in the real number field?

273
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:19,760
Right.

274
00:17:20,020 --> 00:17:30,240
So we are searching for a 3 by 3 matrix filled entirely with positive or negative 1 over root 3, where every single row is orthogonal to every other row.

275
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:32,860
I want to make sure everyone understands orthogonal in this context.

276
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:44,280
If I have two rows of numbers and they are orthogonal, it means if I multiply the matching numbers together and add up the results, taking the dot product, they perfectly cancel each other out to equal 0.

277
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:46,520
That is the exact mathematical hurdle.

278
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:58,920
If we factor out the 1 over root 3 from our hypothetical matrix, what we are actually hunting for is a 3 by 3 matrix consisting entirely of positive ones and negative ones, whose rows dot product to 0.

279
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:03,900
And in mathematical literature, this highly specific object is called a Hadamard matrix, right?

280
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:07,880
And here is the punchline that completely blew my mind when reading this.

281
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:14,100
How many 3 by 3 hadamard matrices actually exist in the entirety of mathematics?

282
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:14,680
Zero.

283
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:15,880
Not a single one.

284
00:18:16,060 --> 00:18:19,340
It is a rigorous, brute force impossibility.

285
00:18:20,220 --> 00:18:21,480
Think about it mechanically.

286
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:25,160
There are 9 entries in a 3 by 3 matrix.

287
00:18:25,660 --> 00:18:28,520
Each entry can only be a plus 1 or a minus 1.

288
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:36,160
Okay, so that means there are exactly 2 to the power of 9 or 512 possible combinations of signs you could arrange.

289
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:36,420
Right.

290
00:18:36,520 --> 00:18:41,580
You can easily program a basic computer script to check all 512 of those combinations.

291
00:18:41,720 --> 00:18:41,880
Yeah.

292
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:46,300
And you will find that exactly 0 of them have rows that are orthogonal to each other.

293
00:18:46,380 --> 00:18:50,000
Wait, I can actually see why that fails just by thinking about the dot product.

294
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:58,380
If I take a row of 3 1s or minus 1s, and I multiply it by another row of 3 1s or minus 1s, I get 3 numbers that are each either plus 1 or minus 1.

295
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:05,340
If I add 3 of those together, I mean, I can get 3, or I can get 1, or I can get minus 1, or I can get minus 3, but I can never, ever get 0.

296
00:19:05,860 --> 00:19:10,880
You need an even number of terms for the plus 1s and minus 1s to perfectly cancel each other out.

297
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:12,820
That is a brilliant realization.

298
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:19,440
The classical mathematics of Hadamard matrices proves this exact intuition more broadly.

299
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,940
They only exist in sizes 1, 2, and multiples of 4.

300
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:28,520
A 3x3 Hadamard matrix is a logical contradiction.

301
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:29,280
Which means?

302
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:37,160
Which means it is fundamentally impossible to generate that uniform 1 third probability transition using only real amplitudes.

303
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:42,920
The real numbers simply do not possess the geometric flexibility to remain strictly orthogonal to each other,

304
00:19:43,100 --> 00:19:47,700
while simultaneously distributing probability equally across three distinct outcomes.

305
00:19:47,900 --> 00:19:49,600
But the complex numbers can't.

306
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:49,780
Yes.

307
00:19:49,920 --> 00:19:53,960
The complex numbers, however, possess a continuous spectrum of rotating phase angles.

308
00:19:54,480 --> 00:19:57,360
They can twist and slide into orthogonal positions effortlessly.

309
00:19:57,780 --> 00:19:58,060
Okay.

310
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:03,300
I have a massive pushback here, and it's something I grappled with when I first read the text.

311
00:20:03,720 --> 00:20:07,880
When my physics brain kicked in, I thought, okay, sure, complex numbers win here.

312
00:20:07,880 --> 00:20:10,380
But isn't that just incredibly obvious?

313
00:20:10,380 --> 00:20:12,880
Isn't that simply because of interference?

314
00:20:13,360 --> 00:20:13,840
Interference?

315
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:14,840
Yeah.

316
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:21,240
We are taught that when you square quantum amplitudes to get probabilities, you lose the cross terms.

317
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:25,200
You lose the interference patterns that dictate how waves interact.

318
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:30,840
So obviously, if we drop down to a layer where we only look at probabilities, we have permanently lost information,

319
00:20:30,840 --> 00:20:36,080
and therefore we must have complex amplitudes under the hood to keep track of that lost interference.

320
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:36,360
Right.

321
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:39,440
Isn't the necessity of interference the reason real numbers fail?

322
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:42,680
That is a very common and very standard intuition in physics.

323
00:20:42,680 --> 00:20:48,320
But the author dedicates Chapter 4 to proving exactly why that intuition is fundamentally incomplete.

324
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:51,500
What you are describing is known as the composition obstruction.

325
00:20:51,920 --> 00:20:58,400
It is undeniably true that when you compose two processes at the amplitude level and then square them down to get probabilities,

326
00:20:58,760 --> 00:21:06,340
you generate interference cross terms that you absolutely would not get if you had just multiplied the probabilities directly.

327
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:06,880
Right.

328
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:14,740
The whole probability of event A, then event B happening is not just the probability of A times the probability of B phenomenon.

329
00:21:15,080 --> 00:21:17,220
The paths interfere with each other.

330
00:21:17,320 --> 00:21:17,620
Exactly.

331
00:21:17,960 --> 00:21:23,320
Information is demonstrably lost when you project from the world of amplitudes down to the world of probabilities.

332
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:32,760
However, Chapter 4 explicitly and rigorously proves that this loss of interference information happens in both the complex theory and the real theory.

333
00:21:32,880 --> 00:21:33,040
Wait.

334
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:35,220
Real numbers experience interference too.

335
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:36,320
They absolutely do.

336
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:41,800
If you compose two real orthogonal matrices together and then square the resulting entries to get probabilities,

337
00:21:42,100 --> 00:21:45,120
you produce the exact same cross-term interference structure.

338
00:21:45,380 --> 00:21:47,660
You lose information in the exact same manner.

339
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:48,300
Oh, wow.

340
00:21:48,520 --> 00:21:48,640
Yeah.

341
00:21:48,700 --> 00:21:54,540
The fact that projecting down to probability destroys interference does not single out complex numbers as uniquely necessary.

342
00:21:54,820 --> 00:21:58,940
The physical need for interference is equally well satisfied by real amplitudes.

343
00:21:59,260 --> 00:22:01,400
So my intuition was entirely backwards.

344
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:08,080
The catastrophic failure of real numbers at n equals 3 isn't because real numbers can't do interference.

345
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:10,100
They can do interference just fine.

346
00:22:10,100 --> 00:22:15,140
The failure is a completely separate, deeper geometric inadequacy.

347
00:22:15,300 --> 00:22:15,900
Precisely.

348
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:26,040
The real numbers literally lack the geometric space to build that specific 3x3 orthogonal structure, whereas the complex numbers have the space to accommodate it.

349
00:22:26,040 --> 00:22:27,580
That's the reality of the math.

350
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:36,420
If the existence of interference doesn't prove that we need complex numbers, we have to look elsewhere for the ultimate geometric trap that real numbers fall into.

351
00:22:36,420 --> 00:22:43,520
And to find that trap, we have to stop looking at single matrices and start looking at what happens when you attempt to travel in a circle.

352
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:44,860
Okay, let's unpack this.

353
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:49,120
Chapters 5 and 6 of the text delve into context graphs and cycle consistency.

354
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:57,960
This is where we stop staring at a single isolated transformation and start looking at how different measurement contexts relate to one another in a broader system.

355
00:22:57,960 --> 00:23:03,660
Imagine you have a physical system in front of you, and you have three completely different ways you could choose to measure it.

356
00:23:03,660 --> 00:23:06,660
Let's label these measurement contexts z, x, and y.

357
00:23:06,660 --> 00:23:10,760
Each of these contexts represents a complete set of orthogonal states.

358
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:19,640
If we're trying to force this into a real number theory, you can visualize these contexts as three different rigid coordinate frames.

359
00:23:20,060 --> 00:23:23,760
Three different ways to orient the x and y axes on a flat piece of paper.

360
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:28,080
Like holding a piece of graph paper and rotating it to three different arbitrary angles.

361
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:28,700
Exactly.

362
00:23:28,940 --> 00:23:36,200
Now, suppose you have carefully calibrated your equipment, and you know exactly how context z mathematically relates to context x.

363
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:40,560
You have a precise transition matrix that describes the angle of rotation between them.

364
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:41,040
Got it.

365
00:23:41,140 --> 00:23:46,420
And you know exactly how context x relates to context y, and finally you know how y relates back to z.

366
00:23:46,420 --> 00:23:53,560
Well, if we were looking at the pure upper level underlying amplitude math, the full information unsquared matrices, this should be a trivial exercise, right?

367
00:23:53,760 --> 00:24:02,120
If you mathematically transform your coordinates from z to x, and then from x to y, and then from y back to z, you have just walked in a complete closed circle.

368
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:10,920
In that exact upper level amplitude math, multiplying those three transformation matrices together must exactly equal the identity matrix.

369
00:24:11,420 --> 00:24:13,840
You are right back to where you started with no changes.

370
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:19,300
The matrix LZx multiplied by Lxy multiplied by Lyz equals I.

371
00:24:19,740 --> 00:24:21,920
The mathematical loop closes perfectly.

372
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:25,920
But the whole point of this exercise is that we don't live in the upper level math.

373
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:27,740
We live in a physical laboratory.

374
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:32,180
We only ever see the squared probabilities clicking on our detectors.

375
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:34,940
We only see the shadows dancing on the cave wall.

376
00:24:35,260 --> 00:24:37,280
And that is the crux of the entire problem.

377
00:24:38,040 --> 00:24:48,080
When you are forced to drop down to probability observations, when you square those delicate transition matrices to get your observable probability grids, you irreversibly lose information.

378
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:51,840
How much information are we actually losing in this specific scenario?

379
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:56,440
The author calculates this with pinpoint precision in what they call the triangle gluing theorem.

380
00:24:57,120 --> 00:25:06,600
When you strip away the underlying signs and phases, and you are only allowed to look at the raw transition probabilities between these 2D frames, you lose exactly one bit of information per loop.

381
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:08,860
Specifically, you lose a relative sign.

382
00:25:08,860 --> 00:25:13,380
I was trying to visualize this when reading, and I kept coming back to a woodshop analogy.

383
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:24,620
Imagine I give you three pieces of wood, and I tell you the exact angles you need to cut on the ends of each piece so that they will fit together to form a perfect closed triangle.

384
00:25:25,020 --> 00:25:26,020
Okay, I can picture that.

385
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:27,620
You take piece C and piece X.

386
00:25:27,940 --> 00:25:29,840
You cut the joint, and they fit perfectly.

387
00:25:30,380 --> 00:25:33,440
You take X and Y, cut the joint, and they fit flawlessly.

388
00:25:33,860 --> 00:25:37,820
You go to connect the final joint, bringing Y back to Z.

389
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:38,660
And it doesn't fit.

390
00:25:38,720 --> 00:25:39,540
It doesn't fit.

391
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:52,080
Because if you are forced to keep all three pieces of wood completely flat on the workbench, which physically represents being trapped in the flat 2D real number plane, that final corner physically cannot meet.

392
00:25:52,380 --> 00:25:55,980
That woodshop analogy is an exceptional way to visualize the constraint.

393
00:25:56,280 --> 00:26:05,960
The angles that looked perfectly valid and mathematically sound individually are globally inconsistent when you force them to form a closed loop on a restricted flat surface.

394
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:06,820
Exactly.

395
00:26:06,820 --> 00:26:14,540
The individual transition probabilities between the contexts might be perfectly valid, perfectly orthostochastic on their own.

396
00:26:14,940 --> 00:26:26,000
But when you attempt to assign a single globally consistent set of real number axes to all three contexts simultaneously, the math snags on that lost bit of sign information.

397
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:31,820
And the author provides the absolute sharpest, most painful example of this mathematical snag in action.

398
00:26:32,260 --> 00:26:36,160
The attempt to build mutually unbiased bases, or MUBs.

399
00:26:36,440 --> 00:26:40,780
Mutually unbiased bases are a foundational concept in quantum information theory.

400
00:26:41,300 --> 00:26:53,900
If two bases are mutually unbiased, it means that if you prepare a system perfectly in a state from the first basis and then measure it using the second basis, you have a perfectly uniform, totally random probability of getting any of the possible outcome.

401
00:26:53,900 --> 00:27:00,260
It's the ultimate uncertainty. If I know exactly how fast a particle is moving and I measure its position, the result is completely random.

402
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:01,740
They are mutually unbiased.

403
00:27:01,800 --> 00:27:09,380
Right. Now, take that concept and imagine trying to construct three measurement contexts, Z, X, and Y, that are all pairwise mutually unbiased.

404
00:27:09,380 --> 00:27:14,600
Z is unbiased relative to X, X is unbiased to Y, and Y is unbiased back to Z.

405
00:27:14,820 --> 00:27:29,960
If you attempt to mathematically build this triad using only real numbers in a two-dimensional space R2, the triangle gluing theorem demands that a highly specific geometric equation must hold true for the loop to close.

406
00:27:29,960 --> 00:27:40,780
I remember staring at this equation in the text. It forces the real number of math into a corner where it has to mathematically declare that the angle pi over 4 is congruent to 0.

407
00:27:41,160 --> 00:27:46,300
Which is, of course, a complete absurdity. Pi over 4 is 45 degrees. It does not equal 0.

408
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:47,120
Obviously not.

409
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:56,680
The real plane R2 is too rigid, too flat. It simply cannot hold three mutually unbiased bases simultaneously. The mathematical loop physically cannot close.

410
00:27:56,680 --> 00:28:06,120
But going back to the woodshop, if you lift one of those pieces of wood slightly off the flat table, if you allow it to move into the third dimension, you can easily bend it so the corners touch perfectly.

411
00:28:06,280 --> 00:28:15,080
And in our mathematical framework, lifting it off the table corresponds directly to moving from the restrictive real numbers R2 to the more expansive complex number C2.

412
00:28:15,260 --> 00:28:16,080
Ah, I see.

413
00:28:16,280 --> 00:28:19,260
The complex numbers provide those extra degrees of freedom.

414
00:28:19,260 --> 00:28:22,920
The imaginary phase angles, which act as that third dimension.

415
00:28:23,360 --> 00:28:27,020
They allow the math to twist the pieces and seamlessly close the loop.

416
00:28:27,600 --> 00:28:34,660
The complex theory handles those exact same three mutually unbiased bases flawlessly without any absurdities.

417
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:40,580
Chapter 7 wraps up this entire exploration by formally calling this triangle the minimal witness.

418
00:28:40,580 --> 00:28:44,300
In graph theory terms, the triangle represents a cycle rank of 1.

419
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:55,960
It is the absolute smallest possible structure, the minimal witness, where real numbers definitively fail to map physical reality and complex numbers elegantly succeed.

420
00:28:56,280 --> 00:29:04,200
So if we pause and take stock, we've seen a single isolated system get twisted into a logical knot if we try to force it to use real numbers.

421
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:12,260
It fails structurally at n equals 3 with a single matrix, and it fails at n equals 2 if we attempt to chain three different contexts together in a closed loop.

422
00:29:12,460 --> 00:29:15,320
Real numbers just can't hack the geometry of the universe.

423
00:29:15,460 --> 00:29:23,400
But the author is not even close to finish putting the constraints on trial, because there's a whole other axis to physical reality that we haven't even touched yet.

424
00:29:23,420 --> 00:29:23,580
Right.

425
00:29:23,800 --> 00:29:27,280
The universe isn't just one isolated system sitting alone in a void.

426
00:29:27,820 --> 00:29:28,700
Things interact.

427
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:33,560
What happens when you bring a second entirely separate system into the mix?

428
00:29:33,560 --> 00:29:39,980
This takes us to section 4 of our deep dive, the dimensions of reality and system composition.

429
00:29:40,540 --> 00:29:42,500
This is where chapter 8 opens up.

430
00:29:43,140 --> 00:29:50,920
Imagine you have system A, let's say an electron, sitting in a laboratory in New York, and you have system B, another electron, sitting in a laboratory in Tokyo.

431
00:29:51,120 --> 00:29:51,360
Okay.

432
00:29:51,940 --> 00:30:00,560
The fundamental question is how do their individual mathematical descriptions combine to describe the joint overarching system of A and B together?

433
00:30:00,560 --> 00:30:07,360
Now, any standard physics textbook would just scream, use the tensor product and move on to the next page without a second thought.

434
00:30:07,440 --> 00:30:08,840
That's standard operating procedure.

435
00:30:09,360 --> 00:30:12,880
But remember our guiding motto, constraint before content.

436
00:30:13,060 --> 00:30:17,000
We're intentionally not assuming the complex tensor product right out of the gate.

437
00:30:17,200 --> 00:30:20,160
We are rigorously testing the candidate's scalar fields.

438
00:30:20,160 --> 00:30:27,500
So we are going to put the real numbers, the complex numbers, and even the quaternions on trial and see how they handle the stress of composite systems.

439
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:28,060
Okay.

440
00:30:28,380 --> 00:30:28,820
Quaternions.

441
00:30:28,940 --> 00:30:29,960
We haven't even mentioned them yet.

442
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:35,640
Those are the exotic math structures that are like complex numbers on steroids, right?

443
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:39,620
Instead of just one imaginary part, they have three different imaginary parts, I, J, and K.

444
00:30:39,620 --> 00:30:40,380
That's a good summary.

445
00:30:40,780 --> 00:30:49,200
They are a perfectly valid, robust mathematical field, and they are often considered by physicists as a candidate for building exotic physical theories.

446
00:30:49,420 --> 00:30:53,540
So the author tests all three, real complex and quaternionic.

447
00:30:53,620 --> 00:30:58,420
And the strict constraint we apply to test them is a principle called local tomography?

448
00:30:58,760 --> 00:30:59,100
Yes.

449
00:30:59,400 --> 00:31:08,520
The text defines this as the strict rule that a joint state should be exactly as informative as the sum of all complete local measurements on its individual parts.

450
00:31:08,520 --> 00:31:23,440
Meaning, if I completely measure every single thing there is to know about System A in New York, and you completely measure every single thing there is to know about System B in Tokyo, and we get on the phone and pool our data, we should have the complete 100% picture of the joint system A-B.

451
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:33,020
There should be no holistic parameters, no spooky hidden variables that exist only in the joint system that we can't see by looking really closely at the individual parts.

452
00:31:33,380 --> 00:31:35,740
The parts must fully explain the whole.

453
00:31:35,740 --> 00:31:46,060
To test if this holds true, the author uses a stunning piece of dimension-counting mathematics, often referred to in the literature as the Iraqi Woodard's Hardy Argument.

454
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:54,380
We're literally going to count the degrees of freedom, the available dimensions of the state spaces for these different mathematical fields when they compose.

455
00:31:54,640 --> 00:31:56,400
Let's do the math out loud for the listener.

456
00:31:56,540 --> 00:32:00,120
Let's start with what turns out to be the Goldilocks case, complex number C.

457
00:32:00,120 --> 00:32:06,760
In a complex mathematical theory, the dimension of the state space for a system of size D is exactly D-squared.

458
00:32:07,220 --> 00:32:09,780
So System A in New York has a dimension of D-A-squared.

459
00:32:10,280 --> 00:32:13,340
System B in Tokyo has a dimension of D-B-squared.

460
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:13,640
Okay.

461
00:32:13,980 --> 00:32:20,340
If the principle of local tomography holds true, multiplying the information of the parts should give us the exact information of the whole.

462
00:32:20,780 --> 00:32:23,400
So we multiply D-A-squared times D-B-squared.

463
00:32:23,400 --> 00:32:26,280
Which gives us D-A-times D-B-squared.

464
00:32:26,700 --> 00:32:36,520
And if we build a brand new complex system from scratch with the size of D-A-times D-B, its state space dimension formula tells us it is also D-A-times D-B-squared.

465
00:32:36,700 --> 00:32:37,900
It perfectly balances.

466
00:32:38,140 --> 00:32:38,540
Exactly.

467
00:32:38,840 --> 00:32:41,720
The parts multiplied together exactly equal the dimensions of the whole.

468
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:45,580
Complex numbers satisfy the constraint of local tomography flawlessly.

469
00:32:46,040 --> 00:32:47,580
The ledger is perfectly balanced.

470
00:32:47,860 --> 00:32:50,660
But what happens when we force quaternions to compose?

471
00:32:50,660 --> 00:32:54,120
For quaternions H, the state space dimension formula is different.

472
00:32:54,260 --> 00:32:56,440
It is D-times-2-D-minus-1.

473
00:32:56,620 --> 00:33:00,340
Let's test this by composing two basic quibits, so our size D equals 2.

474
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:00,660
Okay.

475
00:33:00,860 --> 00:33:07,400
The local dimension for one system is 2 times 4 minus 1, which is 2 times 3, giving us 6 dimensions of data.

476
00:33:07,560 --> 00:33:09,640
So System A in New York has 6 dimensions.

477
00:33:09,900 --> 00:33:11,540
System B in Tokyo has 6.

478
00:33:11,920 --> 00:33:14,700
If we multiply them together, 6 times 6 equals 36.

479
00:33:15,200 --> 00:33:19,820
Local tomography predicts that the joint overarching system should have exactly 36 dimensions.

480
00:33:19,820 --> 00:33:25,440
But if we look at the actual quaternionic state space formula for a joint system of size 4,

481
00:33:25,980 --> 00:33:32,600
since 2 times 2 equals 4 outcomes total, the dimension formula is 4 times 8 minus 1,

482
00:33:33,020 --> 00:33:35,760
which is 4 times 7, resulting in 28 dimensions.

483
00:33:36,060 --> 00:33:38,460
36 predicted versus 28 actual.

484
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:45,300
The local parts give you 36 dimensions of data, but the joint system mathematically only has room to hold 28.

485
00:33:45,300 --> 00:33:47,800
The composite space has too few dimensions.

486
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:51,140
The local parts mathematically over-determine the whole.

487
00:33:51,680 --> 00:33:56,700
The quaternionic tensor product is a famously messy, over-constrained mathematical object,

488
00:33:56,980 --> 00:34:02,600
and this simple dimension counting exercise shows exactly why it fails the test of physical reality.

489
00:34:02,680 --> 00:34:05,020
Okay, so quaternions are violently thrown out.

490
00:34:05,160 --> 00:34:07,260
What about our old frenemy, the real numbers?

491
00:34:07,260 --> 00:34:12,280
For real numbers are, the state space dimension formula is d times d plus 1 divided by 2.

492
00:34:12,720 --> 00:34:15,620
Let's test composing two real quavats, so again, d equals 2.

493
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:20,860
The local dimension for New York is 2 times 3 divided by 2, which simplifies to 3 dimensions.

494
00:34:21,240 --> 00:34:23,440
System A provides 3 dimension of data.

495
00:34:23,860 --> 00:34:25,080
System B provides 3.

496
00:34:25,560 --> 00:34:26,520
Multiply them together.

497
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:28,260
3 times 3 equals 9.

498
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:33,360
Local tomography predicts exactly 9 dimensions for the joint real system.

499
00:34:33,360 --> 00:34:37,920
Now, calculate the actual real state space for a joint system of size 4.

500
00:34:38,560 --> 00:34:43,000
The formula is 4 times 5 divided by 2, which equals 10.

501
00:34:43,140 --> 00:34:44,840
9 predicted versus 10 actual.

502
00:34:45,160 --> 00:34:47,760
The real composite space has too many dimensions.

503
00:34:47,940 --> 00:34:49,040
That's the fatal flaw.

504
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:53,920
A real composite system carries extra non-locally accessible parameters.

505
00:34:54,240 --> 00:34:57,420
Okay, we need a really solid analogy here because this is wild.

506
00:34:57,660 --> 00:35:00,300
The last time I tried to explain this with bank accounts, it fell apart.

507
00:35:00,760 --> 00:35:02,040
Let's think about data storage.

508
00:35:02,040 --> 00:35:08,160
Imagine New York has a hard drive holding system A, and Tokyo has a hard drive holding system B.

509
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:08,980
I like this better.

510
00:35:09,080 --> 00:35:09,360
Go on.

511
00:35:09,740 --> 00:35:13,120
With complex numbers, New York has a drive with 10 gigabytes of data.

512
00:35:13,640 --> 00:35:15,360
Tokyo has a drive with 10 gigabytes.

513
00:35:15,860 --> 00:35:21,700
When you network them together, the network administrator sees exactly 100 gigabytes of possible combined states.

514
00:35:22,060 --> 00:35:22,720
It balances.

515
00:35:23,200 --> 00:35:25,900
But with real numbers, New York has a 3 gigabyte drive.

516
00:35:26,380 --> 00:35:27,760
Tokyo has a 3 gigabyte drive.

517
00:35:27,760 --> 00:35:34,300
When they network them together, the network administrator suddenly sees 10 gigabytes of data space available.

518
00:35:34,800 --> 00:35:39,420
There is an entire phantom gigabyte of data that physically exists in the network.

519
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:44,020
But if you inspect the New York drive or the Tokyo drive individually, it is nowhere to be found.

520
00:35:44,200 --> 00:35:45,680
That analogy holds up perfectly.

521
00:35:45,680 --> 00:35:53,900
The local observers in New York and Tokyo literally cannot access or see the extra parameter that exists in the joint reality.

522
00:35:54,360 --> 00:35:56,320
The parts do not fully explain the whole.

523
00:35:56,720 --> 00:36:01,260
So real numbers actually saddle us with more hidden dimensions than complex numbers do.

524
00:36:01,500 --> 00:36:05,400
That feels entirely backward to how we normally discuss quantum weirdness.

525
00:36:05,620 --> 00:36:08,900
We usually think of complex numbers as the exotic, complicated ones.

526
00:36:09,040 --> 00:36:12,140
What exactly is this invisible 10th dimension in the real numbers?

527
00:36:12,340 --> 00:36:14,400
Where is the hidden gigabyte of data hiding?

528
00:36:14,400 --> 00:36:21,180
To answer what this phantom dimension actually is, we have to isolate the exact matrix where real numbers hide their secrets.

529
00:36:21,600 --> 00:36:25,240
And that brings us to section 5 of our dive, the invisible thread.

530
00:36:25,560 --> 00:36:27,160
This covers chapters 9 through 12.

531
00:36:27,620 --> 00:36:29,960
And this is where the text gets incredibly concrete.

532
00:36:30,360 --> 00:36:34,520
It doesn't just wave its hands and say there is a 10th dimension somewhere in the math.

533
00:36:34,720 --> 00:36:40,100
It literally hunts down and isolates the exact matrix responsible for it.

534
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:40,600
It does.

535
00:36:41,120 --> 00:36:46,500
And the culprit responsible for the missing dimension is an antisymmetric matrix conventionally called J.

536
00:36:46,800 --> 00:36:48,780
Let's define antisymmetric for the listener.

537
00:36:49,100 --> 00:36:53,120
If you have a square grid of numbers and you flip it across its diagonal axis,

538
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:59,580
an antisymmetric matrix is one where all the positive numbers become negative and all the negative numbers become positive.

539
00:36:59,580 --> 00:37:00,900
That is the exact definition.

540
00:37:00,900 --> 00:37:08,500
Now, in the real number theory, an observable property of a system, meaning something you can actually build a physical machine in a lab to measure,

541
00:37:08,860 --> 00:37:11,900
must mathematically be represented by a symmetric matrix.

542
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:12,240
Right.

543
00:37:13,340 --> 00:37:19,060
Because J is antisymmetric, it fundamentally is not a valid observable on its own.

544
00:37:19,360 --> 00:37:23,080
You cannot build a detector in New York to measure J on system A.

545
00:37:23,280 --> 00:37:25,580
It is completely invisible to local measurement.

546
00:37:25,580 --> 00:37:28,400
Okay, so J doesn't exist locally, but...

547
00:37:28,400 --> 00:37:32,020
Well, what happens mathematically when you multiply two antisymmetric matrices together?

548
00:37:32,400 --> 00:37:34,260
A negative times a negative is a positive.

549
00:37:34,460 --> 00:37:34,980
Precisely.

550
00:37:35,340 --> 00:37:39,660
The text proves that when you take the tensor product of the invisible J matrix on system A

551
00:37:39,660 --> 00:37:45,280
and multiply it by the invisible J matrix on system B, creating the joint matrix J tensor J,

552
00:37:45,980 --> 00:37:48,960
the antisymmetry of the two parts perfectly cancels out.

553
00:37:49,220 --> 00:37:52,560
The resulting joint matrix is completely mathematically symmetric.

554
00:37:52,560 --> 00:38:00,200
Oh, wow. Because it is now symmetric J tensor, J becomes a real, valid, observable property of the joint overarching system.

555
00:38:00,420 --> 00:38:05,380
But because you can only build it by multiplying two things that don't locally exist as observables...

556
00:38:05,380 --> 00:38:08,260
No local product-based measurement can ever possibly see it.

557
00:38:08,720 --> 00:38:12,160
The local scientists in New York and Tokyo are completely blind to its existence,

558
00:38:12,540 --> 00:38:14,940
but it physically exists in the joint reality.

559
00:38:15,600 --> 00:38:19,700
That J tensor J matrix is exactly your phantom tenth dimension.

560
00:38:19,700 --> 00:38:23,640
That is deeply unsettling. If this parameter exists, does it have bounds?

561
00:38:23,920 --> 00:38:26,280
Can this invisible dimension be cranked up to infinity?

562
00:38:26,780 --> 00:38:30,000
It cannot, and that boundary is covered in Chapter 10, Positivity.

563
00:38:30,740 --> 00:38:37,120
The fundamental mathematics of probability strictly requires that all probabilities must be greater than or equal to zero.

564
00:38:37,480 --> 00:38:39,960
You can't have a negative 10% chance of something happening.

565
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:40,540
Right.

566
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:44,900
This positivity constraint places a strict leech on our hidden dimension.

567
00:38:45,620 --> 00:38:52,280
If we add a variable amount of this J tensor J matrix to our overall state, let's call the amount cc,

568
00:38:52,680 --> 00:38:59,900
the positivity constraint proves mathematically that the parameter c must be strictly bounded between minus 1 and 1.

569
00:38:59,900 --> 00:39:02,160
So it's a genuine physical sliding scale.

570
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:09,400
You can have a universe where the state is c equals 0.5 or a universe where c equals minus 0.8,

571
00:39:09,540 --> 00:39:13,400
and they are genuinely, measurably different global states of reality.

572
00:39:13,520 --> 00:39:15,660
They are completely distinct global states, yes.

573
00:39:16,220 --> 00:39:20,000
But look at what happens in Chapter 11 when we introduce the partial trace.

574
00:39:20,240 --> 00:39:25,200
The partial trace is the mathematical tool physicists use for ignoring one part of a system, right?

575
00:39:25,200 --> 00:39:28,520
If I'm sitting in New York and I want to predict what my local instruments will do,

576
00:39:28,940 --> 00:39:32,620
I use the partial trace to mathematically erase Tokyo from the equations.

577
00:39:33,220 --> 00:39:38,260
And when you apply that partial trace operation to our global state containing the hidden c parameter,

578
00:39:38,860 --> 00:39:42,000
the mathematics completely annihilates the J tensor J term.

579
00:39:42,140 --> 00:39:44,900
The c parameter vanishes entirely from the equation.

580
00:39:45,160 --> 00:39:49,220
So no matter what the global value of c actually is,

581
00:39:49,620 --> 00:39:52,660
whether the universe is sitting at 0.9 or minus 0.5,

582
00:39:52,660 --> 00:39:57,440
the state looks totally, completely mixed and boring to the local observer in New York.

583
00:39:57,700 --> 00:39:59,220
It just looks like random static.

584
00:39:59,380 --> 00:39:59,820
Exactly.

585
00:40:00,120 --> 00:40:04,900
The entire physical interval of states between minus 1 and 1 is marginally invisible.

586
00:40:05,080 --> 00:40:06,520
You cannot tell them apart locally.

587
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:11,180
Now this realization sets up the absolute biggest twist in the entire text.

588
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:11,460
Yeah.

589
00:40:11,700 --> 00:40:13,760
Chapter 12, the mind bender.

590
00:40:13,960 --> 00:40:14,460
Oh, I'm ready.

591
00:40:14,800 --> 00:40:19,500
We have this state, let's call it row of c, containing this invisible parameter c.

592
00:40:19,940 --> 00:40:22,100
We have to ask the ultimate quantum question.

593
00:40:22,100 --> 00:40:24,020
Is this state entangled?

594
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:24,900
Entangled.

595
00:40:25,360 --> 00:40:27,300
The signature feature of quantum mechanics,

596
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:33,060
the spooky non-local correlation that cannot possibly be explained by classical shared information.

597
00:40:33,080 --> 00:40:33,560
That's the one.

598
00:40:33,660 --> 00:40:36,560
So we ask, the math is the state row of c, entangled.

599
00:40:36,820 --> 00:40:39,700
And the text delivers a spectacular unexpected answer.

600
00:40:40,120 --> 00:40:44,180
It proves that the answer depends entirely on which mathematical field you're using to look at it.

601
00:40:44,260 --> 00:40:44,620
Wait, what?

602
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:46,640
Entanglement is a physical reality.

603
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:47,960
It shouldn't depend on the math.

604
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:49,040
Let's look at the proof.

605
00:40:49,040 --> 00:40:53,620
If you are operating strictly within the theory of real numbers,

606
00:40:54,500 --> 00:41:01,260
the text proves definitively that the state is entangled for any value of c other than exactly zero.

607
00:41:01,960 --> 00:41:06,040
The J tensor J matrix literally acts as an entanglement witness.

608
00:41:06,660 --> 00:41:11,020
The correlations it produces absolutely cannot be built out of local real states.

609
00:41:11,020 --> 00:41:15,300
Okay, so if we live in a real number universe, the state is undeniably entangled.

610
00:41:15,420 --> 00:41:22,580
But if you take that exact same physical matrix, that exact same grid of numbers describing the exact same physical correlations,

611
00:41:22,920 --> 00:41:25,500
and you look at it from within the theory of complex numbers.

612
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:29,360
Where complex phases are allowed to exist locally on the individual drives.

613
00:41:29,360 --> 00:41:35,840
The text proves mathematically that the state can be written perfectly as a convex combination of local product states.

614
00:41:36,480 --> 00:41:39,240
Meaning, in the complex theory, it is entirely separable.

615
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:40,780
It is not entangled at all.

616
00:41:40,920 --> 00:41:41,880
So what does this all mean?

617
00:41:42,420 --> 00:41:46,520
It means that entanglement is not an intrinsic fundamental physical property of a matrix.

618
00:41:47,220 --> 00:41:51,280
It is not some glowing magical aura that a state either has or doesn't have in a vacuum.

619
00:41:51,820 --> 00:41:53,160
Entanglement is field relative.

620
00:41:53,160 --> 00:42:00,920
A state is only classified as entangled relative to the mathematical vocabulary you are allowed to use to describe the local parts.

621
00:42:01,300 --> 00:42:02,960
You've grasped the core revelation.

622
00:42:03,780 --> 00:42:07,760
Because the complex theory allows for a much richer, more expansive set of local states,

623
00:42:08,200 --> 00:42:10,600
states utilizing complex rotating phases,

624
00:42:11,120 --> 00:42:16,700
it can locally explain correlations that the impoverished real theory has to throw its hands up

625
00:42:16,700 --> 00:42:20,120
and classify as spooky non-local entanglement.

626
00:42:20,200 --> 00:42:21,060
Oh, wow.

627
00:42:21,060 --> 00:42:26,560
The real theory is forced to call it entanglement simply because its local vocabulary is too poor

628
00:42:26,560 --> 00:42:28,540
to describe the correlation any other way.

629
00:42:28,660 --> 00:42:30,860
That just, that completely breaks my brain a little bit.

630
00:42:30,940 --> 00:42:34,660
It turns entanglement into a feature of the mathematical framework's limitations,

631
00:42:34,900 --> 00:42:37,440
not just a standalone physical fact of the universe.

632
00:42:37,780 --> 00:42:39,880
It brilliantly highlights why the author's motto,

633
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:43,680
Constraint Before Content, is such a powerful and necessary lens.

634
00:42:44,220 --> 00:42:48,500
It shows us exactly where our deeply held physical concepts actually originate.

635
00:42:48,500 --> 00:42:50,880
They originate from the geometry of the math.

636
00:42:51,060 --> 00:42:55,920
So the text has thoroughly and systematically dismantled real numbers.

637
00:42:56,480 --> 00:42:59,100
It showed they fail structurally at n equals 3.

638
00:42:59,600 --> 00:43:02,500
They physically fail to close triangular loops,

639
00:43:02,620 --> 00:43:07,600
and they spawn invisible dimensions that lead to massive field-relative entanglement confusion.

640
00:43:08,400 --> 00:43:11,560
Complex numbers navigate all of these constraints perfectly, but...

641
00:43:11,560 --> 00:43:12,840
But we have a lingering problem.

642
00:43:12,840 --> 00:43:15,160
We still don't have actual physics.

643
00:43:15,160 --> 00:43:20,260
We have spent all this time talking about abstract vector spaces, simplices, and polytopes.

644
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:24,000
How do we get the actual textbook physics equations back?

645
00:43:24,280 --> 00:43:29,540
How do we bridge the gap from geometric constraints to the things I actually learned in college?

646
00:43:29,540 --> 00:43:32,800
That bridge is the final stage of our journey today.

647
00:43:33,080 --> 00:43:34,820
Section 6, earning the textbook.

648
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:39,060
And we cross this bridge through what the author calls the assumption audit.

649
00:43:39,260 --> 00:43:41,580
Part 3 of the text, recovered structure.

650
00:43:41,820 --> 00:43:43,700
I genuinely loved this part of the document.

651
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:47,400
The author is incredibly strict and intellectually honest with themselves.

652
00:43:47,400 --> 00:43:49,880
They refuse to just wave their hands at the end and say,

653
00:43:50,040 --> 00:43:51,700
and therefore quantum mechanics is true.

654
00:43:52,240 --> 00:43:55,920
They painstakingly go through an assumption ledger in chapters 13 and 18

655
00:43:55,920 --> 00:44:01,040
to prove exactly what mathematical rules it costs to rebuild the textbook from scratch.

656
00:44:01,160 --> 00:44:03,240
It's a master class in intellectual honesty.

657
00:44:04,100 --> 00:44:07,540
To rebuild the entirety of finite dimensional quantum mechanics,

658
00:44:07,980 --> 00:44:13,460
the text demonstrates that we only need to explicitly flag four basic operational assumptions.

659
00:44:13,900 --> 00:44:14,280
Four.

660
00:44:14,280 --> 00:44:16,680
Just four logical rules to build the universe.

661
00:44:17,040 --> 00:44:18,220
Let's walk the listener through them.

662
00:44:18,460 --> 00:44:19,120
Assumption one.

663
00:44:20,140 --> 00:44:20,980
Local tomography.

664
00:44:21,600 --> 00:44:23,840
We explored this heavily just a moment ago.

665
00:44:24,260 --> 00:44:29,180
We assume that the joint state of a composite system should be fully 100% explainable

666
00:44:29,180 --> 00:44:32,420
by making local measurements on its individual parts.

667
00:44:32,620 --> 00:44:32,880
Right.

668
00:44:33,040 --> 00:44:34,340
As we saw with the dimension counting,

669
00:44:34,600 --> 00:44:38,800
insisting on this constraint is what fundamentally forces us into the arms of the complex numbers.

670
00:44:38,920 --> 00:44:43,060
Because complex numbers balance the data ledger perfectly without creating phantom dimensions,

671
00:44:43,060 --> 00:44:46,600
so we assume the physical universe prefers a balanced ledger.

672
00:44:47,000 --> 00:44:48,340
Let's move to assumption two.

673
00:44:48,840 --> 00:44:49,560
Assumption two.

674
00:44:50,680 --> 00:44:54,360
Pure states are operationally represented as frame-completable vectors.

675
00:44:54,820 --> 00:44:55,700
Frame-completable.

676
00:44:55,980 --> 00:44:57,100
Let me try to translate that.

677
00:44:57,240 --> 00:45:00,680
It means if I have a state of maximal knowledge, a pure state,

678
00:45:00,680 --> 00:45:07,140
I can mathematically treat that state as one single axis of a complete orthogonal coordinate system.

679
00:45:07,140 --> 00:45:08,480
That's a perfect translation.

680
00:45:09,240 --> 00:45:15,040
It mathematically identifies a state with a distinguished coordinate axis of a measurement context.

